Z||000000||VISCERAL||06-17-99||10:53 AM||||silicone brake fluid- ok to use?
Z||000001||theogre||06-17-99||07:15 PM||||NO NADA NIENE NYET NEVER EVER(I think I spelled that right)<p>DOT-3 brake juce is NOT compatible with SI based fluid. DO NOT EVER mix them. If you are going to replace DOT3 with SI then you must insure ALL the DOT3 is gone from the system and you will likely have to replace all the seals too.<p>If you want the best brake fluid performance from DOT-3 pugre the old stuff out. DOT3 draws moisture over time and starts to boil at lower temperatures.<p>To make it go faster, compress all the calipers full into the bores, and bleed the longest path on each caliper set. Use a vacuum bleeder to draw out the old fluid. Empty out the master cyl tanks before starting and fill them with new DOT3.<p>Expect to use at least 1 quart of fluid.<p>This should also be done any time a caliper or master cyl is replaced. (Same thing with the clutch.) you don't want residual metal and dirt to eat up the new parts.
Z||000002||batboy||06-17-99||08:40 PM||||Ogre is right. The best way to switch over to silicon brake fluid is during a complete brake overhaul. I did this with my 68 Camaro I'm restoring, but I replaced all the lines, master cylinder, and wheel cylinders. Essentially, the entire brake system was new. Like Ogre says, DO NOT mix different types of brake fluid.
Z||000003||Cooter||06-17-99||09:01 PM||||Instead of silicon- dot5- brake fluid, what about just using a high performance dot 4 fluid? It is compatable with the dot 3 and is more resistant to moisture etc. . .  than dot 3, and you wont need to worry about having every last bit of dot 3 out. <p>To make a long story short- had a friend who had his brake system filled with silicon. Front end shop let a coil spring get away and cut the brake line. They refilled the system with dot 3. Not a pretty picture! It could happen to you, so I suggest staying with conventional fluids.
Z||000004||batboy||06-17-99||09:15 PM||||Cooter, how's Daisy and them Duke boys? Sorry, I just had to say that (haha). But, seriously, that's some very good advice. Also, what happens when your brakes break down in the middle of nowhere and the guy at the counter of the combination greasy spoon diner, parts store, and post office says: "What da hell is silicon brake fluid? Ain't never heard of dat!"
Z||000005||theogre||06-18-99||12:13 AM||||I've heard of DOT4, but have never seen it in use. Anyone got a good link about it? I'd like to find some more to read........ I didn't think you could mix that with DOT3.<p>To be honest, I don't think I've ever managed to boil DOT3, even when it's old. I generally only purge the system after a cylinder change. At least on a street car. Even on my '87 SC which I drive the hell out of. (I've put 17+K on it since last october.) On street cars with brake fade it's usually not the fluid that's the problem.<p>If you are racing/autocrossing then old DOT3 with even a little moisture can be a giant pain in the ass. We used to purge some open wheel race cars several times a season. Even with that DOT3 was easier to deal with than SI. SI is just plain nasty to use. Even with a complete rebuild, some OEM spec seals just don't like it, and don't last as long as they would with DOT3. plus DOT3 was allot cheaper, even when we used a gallon a year.
Z||000006||RFMike||06-18-99||07:08 PM||||I have seen dot3 boil, or possibly the moisture in it.  My brother and I were motorcycling across Nebraska, moving really fast on highway 2, with the temp hanging around 100+ all afternoon.  His break lines passed near the engine, and were inside the air flow path.  He pulled in at a one horse gas station and told me he had no front brake.  Checked the level- full.  Guessed it was boiling, or moisture.  Point is, this was one step from the edge, with no large towns for at least 50 miles.  We got a bottle of dot3, tapped the bleeder, and worked it through.  Solved, but it would have been a bad deal without compatible fluid.<br>Course, if he'd changed it before going on a 3,500 mile trip...  
Z||000007||Phil||06-18-99||08:51 PM||||Oger I believe it's nein
Z||000008||lowCG||06-19-99||12:44 AM||||My only experience with silicon fluid was with a British car(Lockheed) brake system,that everyone said I'd be fussing with constantly;never touched it in three years of use.Will admit,I fouled up the clutch portion by adding dot3,had to flush it.The previous owner didn't tell me about this neat feature.I would definately make silicon fluid a part of a major rebuild,for the $,it's worth it,only at that time.<br>You could also put a warning sticker on the filler caps to ward off any unknowing mechanics.I have to do this on vehicles that aren't  maintained by me(with K&Ns,redline tranny oil,different cams).
